the reason why this  is so important is because this show is  that with characterizing entire  population of cells the results you get  are far different than if you're only  characterized in the periphery of cells  which is what most researchers aren't  doing today so if we're looking at only  the exterior we're gonna think cell  preparation is much higher than it  actually is  is we're characterizing the cells that  are most lucrative on the outside of  these models and the show this a little  bit further here and we break down the  three cell culture model into inner  middle and outer we can sort of see the  casi 7% positive cells in each one of  these different parts of a 3d cell  culture model and we see there's a very  very different response depend upon  where we are within the 3d cell culture  model this is very important for looking  at large molecules like antibodies where  we have a very different response  between the exterior and the interior  and if we're only biased are results  towards the exterior the assay is going  to show a very different result that  what's actually happening and was  probably going to happen within the body  so as a company we focus a lot on 3d  cell culture models and trying to figure  out how to best extract  negative three-dimensional information  from these models and again it really  depends on the type of question that  you're trying to answer and the type of  the model you're trying to work with but  again by looking through those different  parameters on how best to develop your  assay how to minimize the amount of data  that you want to acquire you can develop  a 3d cell culture assay that does both  improve your ability to predict it's  going to hack within the body also  reducing costs and allowing your program  to move forward more quickly so thank  you so much the opportunity to present  if you have any questions at all please  feel free to email me at Michael Johnson  at VisiCalc comm thank you thank you dr.  Johnson for the informative presentation  we will now start the Q&amp;A portion of the  webinar and we'll address some of the  most commonly asked questions by our  viewers if you have any questions you'd  like to ask please do so now just click  on the ask a question drop-down box  located on the far left of your  presentation window type your question  into the box that appears on your screen  and click the send button our speaker  will follow up with your questions via  email all right let's get started our  first question is what is the most  challenging component of executing 3d  cell culture assays I would say the  probably the most difficult part we see  from researchers is depends on the type  of assay they're doing but if they're  doing any kind of imaging based assay we  see the most challenging part is  actually the data processing turning a  bunch of really nice images into  something useful we see this is where  most researchers struggle especially  pharma companies pharma companies have a  really great expertise and what we see  is the biology a lot of times the  imaging is well but when they get to  generating terabytes of data per day  actually distilling that down answering  their specific research question or  optimizing some type of algorithm to do  that we see a lot of problems there are  a lot of challenges so that's what we've  seen probably most problems for  researchers we've actually recently  develop software to address that so  folks are interested in learning more  about that shoot me an email looking for  beta testers to test out the free  software but doing that answering those  questions and taking really pretty  pictures and turning them into a table  or turn them through a report that's  where you see the  got foremost researchers it's a lot of  data process and it's an expertise that  one of Life Sciences just don't have a  background in computer vision or English  processing all right and how do you  choose the right model for a 3d cell  culture asset this is a common question  that we get is um what models do you  guys have your own assays and the way  that we look at this is we're pretty  agnostic on the model we always ask the  client well what is your specific  throughput cost and validation criteria  because you could run a very simple  assay or you can run a very complex  assay with a really complex model where  you have a lot of validation criteria  you've done a lot of assay the valve  that your models actually performing the  way that it should  but for many researchers they don't need  that so I think it really depends on the  researcher we work very closely with  researchers to try to educate them on  this that there isn't a liver model  there are a varying spectrum of liver  models and the one that's right for you  really just depends I think a lot of  companies are trying to move towards  like we see micro tissues and we see  organoids but there's very light  unlikely to be a small tiny liver in the  dish with the next few years it's really  about finding that liver in a dish like  thing that answers your research  question and also meets your costs and  your throughput criteria so I would say  it's really just working through those  questions and understanding the  limitations these are not yet micro  organs that plate all right we have time  for one more question how do you analyze  your data to characterize your model  this depends on like the type of  question that we're trying to answer but  to give you probably those complex  answer or complex apps that we do is if  we're doing conflicts of images from a  3d cell culture model what we typically  do is we use our imager to generate TIFF  files so generating lots of image files  and then from those image files we've  generated a whole wide range algorithms  to answer research questions that we  commonly do for our clients so for  example my most common ones that we get  is co-localization you want to count the  number of nuclei  